  [1m[36mTask Update (5.4ms)[0m  [1m[33mUPDATE "tasks" SET "updated_at" = '2026-03-16 12:50:50.892678', "description" = 'I think we should not have an organization flag to enable the requisition feature - it should be a level in the user role. Please recompose you answer and make it less technical and more suitable for a product owner.

Thoughtfor 9s

3 of 9 DoneView all

- Add org-level setting to enable position requisition approvals
- Create PositionRequisition model with state machine and associations
- Hook publish guard in Position to require approved requisition based on role policy
- Add minimal UI: role-based setup and approval inbox
- Define role/permission policy for requisition requirement and approver capability

Thoughtfor 4s

I’ve adjusted the approach to be role-based (no organization toggle) and reframed it for product and stakeholders.

What we’re solving

- As an organization owner, I want a recruitment requisition flow before a position can be posted, so we can control headcount and spend.

Roles and permissions

- Creator: Anyone allowed to create a draft position.

- Approver: Users with an “Approver” capability in their role (e.g., Owner, HR Lead, Finance). You decide which roles have this.

- Optional: Delegated approvers at department level (later phase).

How the flow works

- Draft creation

- Recruiter creates a draft position as today.

- If their role requires approval, they see a banner: “Approval required to publish”. A “Request approval” button opens the requisition form.

- Requisition request

- Form collects: job title, department, headcount, budget (optional), justification.

- On submit, approvers are notified and the draft shows “Approval pending”.

- Approve or reject

- Approvers see an inbox of pending requisitions (filters by department if needed).

- Approve: the associated draft can be published immediately.

- Reject: the recruiter is notified with the reason and can resubmit.

- Publish control

- Users whose role requires approval cannot publish unless the linked requisition is approved.

- Users with publish rights and “Approver” capability can publish without requesting, or they can approve and publish in one step.

What changes for users

- Recruiters: One extra step before publishing when their role requires it (quick form + status chip).

- Approvers: A simple inbox to review and decide, with email notifications and one-click actions.

- Owners/Admins: Decide which roles require approval and which roles can approve.

Default configuration

- Out of the box:

- Recruiters require approval before publishing.

- Owners and HR Leads can approve.

- You can adjust which roles require approval and which can approve.

Edge cases and safeguards

- Multiple drafts for the same role/team: each draft needs approval; we’ll show a helpful hint if a similar requisition was recently approved.

- Changes after approval: significant changes (e.g., department or headcount) trigger a “re-approval required” badge.

- Time-bound approvals (optional): approvals can expire after X days.

Reporting and oversight

- Dashboard cards:

- “Pending requisitions” count by department.

- “Time to approval” median and outliers.

- Export: list of approvals with requester, approver, timestamps, and notes.

Phase 2 ideas (optional)

- Multi-step approvals (e.g., Department → Finance → HR).

- Budget caps per department with auto-routing.

- SLA nudges and escalations.

Rollout plan

- Week 1: Enable permissions for roles, add requisition request UI, single-step approval, and publish gating.

- Week 2: Add approver inbox, notifications, and basic reporting.

- Week 3+: Add re-approval on major changes and (optionally) expirations.

Status update: I replaced the organization toggle with a role-based requirement and simplified the flow for a product owner audience, including roles, user experience, and rollout.

- Roles determine who needs approval and who can approve.

- Recruiters request; approvers decide; publish is blocked until approval for those roles.

